<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kumbhalgarh</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kumbhalgarh Fort</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kumbhalgarh is a hill fort in the Aravalli Range north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udaipur">Udaipur</a>, built in the mid-fifteenth century by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rana-kumbha">Rana Kumbha</a> of Mewar. Its walls, more than 30 km long, enclose many temples, and the fort served as a refuge for the rulers of Mewar: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udai-singh-ii">Udai Singh II</a> was hidden there as a child, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharana-pratap">Maharana Pratap</a> was born there in 1540. It is part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hill-forts-of-rajasthan">Hill Forts of Rajasthan</a> World Heritage Site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kumbhalgarh.jpg" alt="Kumbhalgarh" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rajesh Kapoor · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kumbhalgarh_055.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
